🔧 What Are Functional Interfaces?
A functional interface has only one abstract method, and can be used with lambda expressions or method references.

📦 Located in java.util.function package.

Java functional interfaces like Function, Consumer, Predicate, and Supplier.these are the building blocks of functional programming in Java 8 and above.

📘 Common Built-in Functional Interfaces
| Interface        | Takes           | Returns          | Use Case                       |
| ---------------- | --------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------ |
| `Function<T, R>` | One input (`T`) | One output (`R`) | Transform a value              |
| `Consumer<T>`    | One input (`T`) | `void`           | Use a value (e.g., print, log) |
| `Supplier<T>`    | No input        | One output (`T`) | Provide/generate a value       |
| `Predicate<T>`   | One input (`T`) | `boolean`        | Test a condition               |



1️⃣ Function<T, R>
Takes one input and returns one result.
In Java, the Function<T, R> interface from java.util.function represents a function that accepts one argument of type T and returns a result of type R.
>>>> Detailed example is at the end >>>>>>>>>>
Use when you need to transform data.

java
Copy
Edit
Function<String, Integer> lengthFunc = str -> str.length();
System.out.println(lengthFunc.apply("Java")); // Output: 4

2️⃣ Consumer<T>
Takes one input and returns nothing (void).

Use when you need to perform an action (like printing or saving).

java
Copy
Edit
Consumer<String> printer = msg -> System.out.println("Hello " + msg);
printer.accept("World");  // Output: Hello World

3️⃣ Supplier<T>
Takes no input and returns a value.

Use when you need to generate or supply a value.

java
Copy
Edit
Supplier<Double> randomSupplier = () -> Math.random();
System.out.println(randomSupplier.get());  // Output: (some random number)

4️⃣ Predicate<T>
Takes one input and returns a boolean.

Used for filtering or conditions.

java
Copy
Edit
Predicate<Integer> isEven = num -> num % 2 == 0;
System.out.println(isEven.test(4));  // true

